AKT mutations
Protein kinase B is a Ser-Thr kinase that is activated by PI3K-α, and mediates PI3K signalling.47 A major recurrent mutation (Glu17Lys) in the AKT1 gene, which encodes protein kinase B, has been identified in several solid tumours, including breast, colon, and ovarian cancers.48 Overall, the frequency of AKT1 mutations in NSCLC is about 1%,12,49 and they have only been identified in squamous-cell carcinoma, mostly in cohorts of European and US patients.49 The Glu17Lys mutation occurs in the AKT1 pleckstrin homology domain. This mutation alters the phosphoinositide-binding pocket, and leads to PI3Kindependent protein kinase B activation.48 The mutation does not alter sensitivity to ATP-competitive inhibitors to protein kinase B.48 MK2206 (Merck, Whitehouse Station, NJ, USA), an allosteric inhibitor of protein kinase B, is being tested in phase 1 trials (table 2).50
BRAF mutations
B-RAF is a Ser-Thr kinase that links RAS GTPases to downstream proteins of the MAPK family, which control cell proliferation.27 B-RAF is one of three members of the RAF kinase family: A-RAF, B-RAF, and RAF-1 (also known as c-RAF).51 Somatic BRAF mutations were originally identified in melanomas. About 80% of mutations affect the Val600 residue (exon 15) within the kinase domain. In NSCLC, BRAF mutations are found in 1–3% of tumours (figure), most of which are adenocarcinoma.12,51–53 By contrast with melanomas, NSCLC mostly harbour non-Val600Glu mutations (88% of cases),51–53 including the Leu596Val mutation in the kinase domain and the Gly468Ala mutation in the G loop of the activation domain.51 BRAF mutations are mutually exclusive to EGFR and KRAS mutations. Biologically, BRAF mutations are associated with increased kinase activity and lead to constitutive activation of MAPK2 and MAPK3.53 In a mouse model of lung cancer, expression of the BRAF Val600Glu mutation in lung epithelial cells is required for tumour maintenance.54 Multiple B-RAF inhibitors are currently under development (table 2). A promising compound is PLX4032 (Plexxikon, Berkeley, CA, USA), a smallmolecule inhibitor selective for B-RAF. Remarkable
activity has been seen in melanoma positive for the BRAF Val600Glu mutation.55 Another drug, sorafenib, was originally touted as a B-RAF inhibitor, but is in fact a multikinase inhibitor of RAF-1, B-RAF, vascular endothelial growth factor receptors 1, 2, and 3, plateletderived growth factor receptor, CD117 (also known as c-Kit), and receptor tyrosine kinase. Sorafenib has been evaluated in NSCLC, mostly as an antiangiogenesis agent in combination with chemotherapy; the ESCAPE phase 3
