MAP2K1 mutations
MAPKK1 (also known as MEK1) is a Ser-Thr kinase that activates MAPK2 and MAPK3 downstream of B-RAF.57 In cancers, three mutations have been confirmed in MAP2K1—Glu56Pro, Lys57Asn, and Asp67Asn.58 All mutations occur in the non-kinase portion of the protein. Somatic mutations in MAP2K1 have been identified in 1% of NSCLC (figure), predominantly in adenocarcinomas.58 MAP2K1 mutations are mutually exclusive to EGFR, KRAS, HER2, PIK3CA, and BRAF mutations. The Lys57Asn and Gln56Pro mutations lead to gain of function in vitro,58 and cells harbouring these mutations can display sensitivity to the small-molecule non-ATP-competitive MAPKK1 inhibitor, AZD6244 (AstraZeneca, Macclesfield, UK). Other MAP2K1 mutations that confer acquired resistance to AZD6244 have been reported.29 AZD6244 has been assessed for the treatment of NSCLC in a trial that compared the drug with pemetrexed when the two drugs were used as second-line treatment in unselected patients (table 2). The response rate was 5%.59
MET amplification and point mutations
Hepatocyte growth factor receptor (HGFR) is a receptor tyrosine kinase encoded by MET,60 which is located on chromosome 7q21-q31. Amplification of this gene has been associated with secondary resistance to EGFR tyrosine kinase inhibitors through a mechanism termed kinase switch.61,62 Overall, MET amplification has been reported in about 20% of tumours from patients with acquired resistance. In large cohorts of patients with NSCLC not previously treated with EGFR-specific tyrosine kinase inhibitors, MET amplification is seen in 1·4–21·0% of patients.63–65 This variability might be related to differences in methods and cut-off values used to assess and define
amplification. MET amplification occurs in squamous-cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma.64 In adenocarcinoma, MET amplification is independent from KRAS mutations and EGFR amplification.63–65 In-vitro studies show that MET amplification is associated with increased concentrations of phosphorylated HGFR.62 The ability of this activated receptor to initiate tumorigenesis has been shown in transgenic mouse models, in which ectopic expression has been reported.66 Small-hairpin RNA-mediated knock down of HGFR expression induces apoptosis and growth inhibition in cells that show MET amplification, such as H1993 NSCLC cells.67 These data suggest that MET amplification is necessary and sufficient to drive the proliferation of cancer cells. Amplification of HGFR correlates with a poor prognosis in surgically resected NSCLC.65 By contrast with renal and gastric carcinomas, mutations in the kinase domain of MET are rare in NSCLC. In a cohort of 188 lung adenocarcinomas, only three somatic MET mutations were identified: two in exon 13 encoding the juxtamembrane domain (Arg988del and Tyr1021Asn) and one in exon 18 encoding the kinase domain (Gly1260Cys).12 The biological importance of these mutations is currently unknown. In addition, an intronic splice variant leading to exon 14 deletions has been reported in 2–3% of NSCLC tumours in Japanese cohorts.63,68 Preliminary data indicate that this mutation leads to delayed receptor downregulation and increased ligand-mediated proliferation.68 Small-molecule inhibitors of HGFR are being developed.69 PF-02341066 (Pfizer) is being developed as an ALK inhibitor but also inhibits HGFR kinase activity in mutant MET cells.69 Phase 1 trials are in progress with inhibitors targeted towards specific and multiple kinases that are active against HGFR (PF-02341066 [Pfizer], GSK1363089 [ formerly known as XL880], and XL184 [Exelixis, San Francisco, CA, USA]; table 2). AMG 102 (Amgen, Thousand Oaks, CA, USA) is a fully humanised IgG2 monoclonal antibody that binds to and neutralises HGF, which prevents its binding to HGFR. A phase 2 trial of this drug in combination with chemotherapy is underway (table 2).
Application to clinical therapy
The identification of multiple clinically relevant driver mutations in NSCLC has improved the understanding of lung cancer pathogenesis. A major hope is that knowledge of these lesions can be used to improve the care of patients. To this end, multiple academic centres have begun to develop multiplex mutational profiling assays in clinical molecular diagnostic laboratories to genotype tumours prospectively. Results will be used in clinical decision making.70 This review focuses mainly on the EGFR signalling pathway. NSCLCs do, however, harbour alterations in other genes that contribute to lung tumorigenesis but that might not be necessary for tumour maintenance,
Search strategy and selection criteria
Data for this review were identified by searches of Medline, Current Contents, PubMed, and references from relevant articles with the following search terms: “non-small cell lung cancer” AND (“mutation” OR “targeted therapy” OR “ALK” OR “HER2” OR “AKT” OR “PIK3CA” OR “MAPK” OR “MEK” OR “BRAF” OR “MET”). Abstracts and reports from meetings (American Society of Clinical Oncology and American Association for Cancer Research) were also included. Data from the Catalogue of Somatic Mutations in Cancer (COSMIC) of the Sanger Institute and the ClinicalTrials.gov database were also retrieved with the same search terms. Only articles published in English between 1989 and 2009 were included.


